WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.860
- So I've really avoided
making a morning routine video,

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.940
specifically because I
feel like there's just

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.680
so many of them on YouTube,

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.290
and I feel like morning routines
are generally overrated.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.717
Everyone's seen thumbnails
on YouTube of like,

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:15.226
"The One Morning Routine
That Will Change Your Life."

00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:17.493
Actually, that sounds like
something that I would make.

00:00:17.493 --> 00:00:20.090
Elon Musk, morning routine revealed.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.620
The secret morning routine
of every billionaire

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.010
who ever lived, apparently.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.780
They all did this one morning routine

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.340
and it's the reason they're successful.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.403
But at the same time though,

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:30.520
there's a reason I'm making this video.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.760
And it's because if there's
one thing that my life

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.840
has been lacking in the past year or so,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.110
it's been structure.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.740
I feel like a lot of people
have felt the same way.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.810
It's kind of a meme that
people don't really know

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.420
what day it is, half the time,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.180
and I think people are starting to realize

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.837
that it feels good to
have to be somewhere,

00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:51.740
to have responsibilities, to
have appointments and meetings.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.320
It makes you feel more human.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.760
So if our life circumstances
has presented a situation

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.470
where there's way less structure,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.250
I think it's important that
we invite more structure

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.260
and more responsibility
into our life voluntarily.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.530
One of the main ways
that I started to do that

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.700
in my life was by actually
adding a morning routine,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.080
having a game plan every time I wake up,

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.530
and having a sequence of
events that I partake in

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.840
that both make me feel
like I'm starting the day

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.400
on the right foot, and that
I'm contributing to society

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.950
and moving my life forward.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.540
So this video is going over what I do,

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:26.927
and hopefully it'll give you some ideas

00:01:26.927 --> 00:01:29.170
as to what you can start doing

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.680
to add more structure into
your life and gain momentum,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.170
get the ball moving again.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.430
So every morning I wake up at 9:00 a.m.,

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.840
and right off the bat,
that's not that early,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.820
but I'm a huge night owl.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.370
If left to my own devices,

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:45.800
I'll fall asleep around 3:00
a.m. every single night.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.980
So after I wake up, the
most important thing I do,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.500
or not do is I don't look at my phone.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.170
I don't go on social media.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.100
I won't go on Reddit,
I won't go on Facebook.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.250
I won't go on Instagram.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.230
I grab my gray hoodie off the
little closet door in my room

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.810
and I march downstairs.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.670
And after I walked downstairs,
I'll walk into the kitchen

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.500
where I'll start boiling
the water for coffee.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.700
And while that's heating up,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.400
I'll lay down a large glass of cold water.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.820
Allegedly, this gets your
metabolism going or something,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.230
which doesn't really make sense to me,

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.030
because there's no calories in water.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.447
But at the very least,
it kind of wakes you up.

00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:24.220
Cold glass of water in the
morning will wake you up,

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.670
maybe because it makes your
brain cold or something.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.929
Anyways, I'll also down
a athletic greens shake.

00:02:29.929 --> 00:02:32.130
They're not sponsoring
this video or anything,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.730
that's just the brand I'm using right now.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.550
I might switch to another
one. You never know.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.600
And I also have two drops
of this vitamin D complex.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.576
And I use the green shake to wash down

00:02:41.576 --> 00:02:45.200
my medicinal mushroom blend of lions mane,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.970
reishi and cordyceps.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.360
I think that's how you pronounce it.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.290
I don't really know if any
of this shit does anything,

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.960
but I'm a lifelong sucker
for pretending that it does.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.382
So after the water's done boiling,

00:02:54.382 --> 00:02:56.910
I'll use an AeroPress to squeeze

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.730
out a nice juicy cup of coffee.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.120
I think AeroPress makes
really great coffee,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.540
even better than pour over.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.400
And I know I'm triggering
everybody from Portland.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.630
Sometimes if I'm feeling really fancy,

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.690
I'll add a tablespoon of
MCT oil and I will blend it,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.720
so I got this nice
blended MCT, oily coffee.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.017
Allegedly this kind of Bulletproof coffee

00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:20.250
increases gray matter in
the brain or something.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.570
I don't even know if that's true,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.440
but I find that the most useful part

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.110
of making coffee like this,

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.170
is that it helps stave off hunger,

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.660
and hunger is extremely
distracting when I'm writing.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.490
So when all that's done,
I walk into my office,

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.400
I sit down on my chair, I light a candle.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.300
I put my hood up.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.500
I set a timer for one hour, and I write.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.180
And like a monk praying
at the chapel at Dawn,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.290
writing for one hour every single morning

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.885
is something that I'm starting to take

00:03:46.885 --> 00:03:49.330
almost religiously seriously.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.720
So the whole idea around
this odd sequence of events

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.760
is that it's all surrounding
a central activity,

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.560
it's an almost sacred ritualistic act

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.430
that prepares the way for the
main event, which is writing.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.610
And there's a reason
why I've chosen writing

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.920
as the central activity
instead of editing,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.090
or shooting B-roll or
responding to emails.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.010
It's because I've identified writing

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.700
as the backbone of my entire career.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.110
Without me putting pen to
paper or finger to keyboard,

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.720
I will have nothing to talk
about in front of the camera,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.700
which means I'll have nothing
to edit and nothing to upload.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.300
And when I have a script or a set of ideas

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.708
that I think is really tight
and that I'm really proud of,

00:04:28.708 --> 00:04:31.680
I'm infinitely more
excited to talk about it

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.650
and share my thoughts
in front of the camera.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.940
And if I think what I'm sharing

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.630
in front of the camera is super
tight and well thought out,

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.120
then I'm infinitely more likely

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.430
to not procrastinate the
living out of editing it.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:44.910
Even shooting the B-roll is way more fun

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.310
when I know the overall
product is something

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.580
that I'm really proud of.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.310
So the overall fulfillment of my life

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.390
and what I do on a day-to-day basis

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.600
rides pretty heavily on me
doing a good job of the writing.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.810
Unfortunately though,

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.370
writing is also something I hate doing.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:02.930
Ever since I started this channel,

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.680
writing has been the thorn in my side.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.970
It's the thing that I
procrastinate the most by far,

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.040
mostly because compared to
being all cinematic and artsy

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.100
with my B-roll and editing
funny memes into my editing.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.700
Editing funny memes into my editing,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:23.530
writing is super, like
excruciatingly mundane in comparison.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.720
You're just sitting there,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.060
hitting your fingers against plastic,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.730
trying to siphon out some kind of gold,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.600
but it's usually coal.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.110
That's the narrative I
have in my head anyways,

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.420
and it's this narrative
of writing being dreadful

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.880
that adds a lot of fuel to
this feeling of resistance.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.460
The resistance that stops me

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.260
from just sitting down and working.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:46.600
And the longer I put it off,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.210
the more the resistance grows in power.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.640
And before I made writing the first thing

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.340
that I do every single morning,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.880
I'd postpone writing
for as long as possible.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.140
For hours and hours, days, and days,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.880
I would do every single other thing

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.050
other than just sitting down and writing.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.303
But the hilarious thing is,

00:06:03.303 --> 00:06:06.670
it's actually not that
hilarious, it's just sad.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.860
The resistance I feel towards writing

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.180
is a lot more unpleasant
than the writing itself.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.080
Once I start writing, it's
actually quite pleasant,

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.550
and peaceful, and dare I say,
I actually really enjoy it.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.120
You know, it's kind of sucky at first,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.340
maybe for the first five or 10 minutes.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.640
but very soon after I've settled down,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.870
my brain has slowed down
and I get into the flow

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.060
every single time.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.100
It's like clockwork.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:33.430
Which has really made me
realize that a lot of the time,

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.090
the resistance that we feel towards doing

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.412
the most important thing
that we have to do in a day

00:06:38.412 --> 00:06:42.010
is more unpleasant than
just doing the thing itself.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.580
And if we eventually have
to do the thing itself,

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.817
after a long enough timeline,

00:06:45.817 --> 00:06:49.200
then why add all of this
suffering in between

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.340
before we just engage with
the suffering of action?

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.830
It's like suffering twice.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.140
Why suffer twice when
you can just suffer once?

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:57.861
And when you structure your day like this,

00:06:57.861 --> 00:07:00.878
you wake up, you do these
fancy things to set the stage

00:07:00.878 --> 00:07:03.800
for this important thing
that you have to do,

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:04.750
and you get in the habit

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.236
of actually doing this
thing every single morning,

00:07:07.236 --> 00:07:10.850
there's a certain level
of existential security

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:11.960
that you'll start to feel.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.900
You don't have this dark cloud

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.670
looming over your head all the time.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.250
You will live most of the day

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:20.090
as somebody who has
done that thing already,

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.460
rather than someone who
has to do that thing.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.930
Maybe you're working a job
that you don't really like,

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.530
and you have this side hustle

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:29.770
that you keep on fantasizing
about as you're way out,

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.280
schedule one hour to work on it

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.410
every single morning non-negotiable

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.930
or maybe you're enjoying your career,

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:37.240
and you have to show up to work

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.030
in a brick and mortar building.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.830
But usually you have tasks to do,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.893
and you should do the
most important task first

00:07:42.893 --> 00:07:45.570
doing the most important thing

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.550
that you have to do every single morning,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.970
getting that fat monkey off your back

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.330
and throwing it to the side or killing it,

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:53.760
but it's kind of an immortal monkey,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.360
'cause you kill it one day and
it'll show up the next day.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.830
But if you get in the habit of doing that,

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:00.220
you'll start to walk around life

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.520
with the exaggerated swagger
of a monkey-killing psychopath.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:05.930
You'll just feel so good.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.940
And you'll free up so much time later

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.133
